Wire Flutter's overscroll physics and indicators into GPUI's scroll containers (`div` with overflow scroll).

What:
- `gpui::overscroll::Overscroll` (`Bounce`, `Stretch`, `Glow`, `None`) and `GestureTuning::overscroll`, default
  `None` (GPUI's hard stop, so upstream behaviour is unchanged until a platform or app sets it). A container
  overrides it with `StatefulInteractiveElement::overscroll(..)`; `.overscroll(Overscroll::None)` opts it out.
  `uniform_list` starts with `None` (its visible range assumes an in-range offset).
- `overscroll/scroll_position.rs`: the glue, ported from Flutter's `ScrollPosition` (`setPixels`,
  `applyNewDimensions`), `ScrollPositionWithSingleContext` (`applyUserOffset`, `goBallistic`, `goIdle`, `jumpTo`,
  `hold`), `ScrollDragController.end` / `cancel`, `HoldScrollActivity`, `BallisticScrollActivity` and
  `IdleScrollActivity`: per axis, one `Activity` (`Idle`, `Drag` by a touch or a trackpad, `Ballistic`) and one
  `Indicator` (`Off`, `Stretch`, `Glow`), driven by scroll steps (`state × phase → next` in `handle_drag`) and
  frames (`layout`). Drags go through 0012's `apply_physics_to_user_offset` and `apply_boundary_conditions`; the
  release starts 0012's `create_ballistic_simulation` with the container's extents, so a bouncing fling into an
  edge springs past it and back, and a clamping one hands its velocity to the stretch (`absorb_impact`). The
  refused overscroll feeds 0015's indicators. Flutter's activity rules: an offset set from outside while the axis
  moves (`ScrollHandle::set_offset`, `scroll_to_item`, `scroll_to_bottom`) is `jumpTo` (go idle, then ballistic
  from there); a new extent restarts the activity (`applyNewDimensions`: a fling keeps its velocity in the new
  range); a horizontal container with nothing to scroll takes no drag (`shouldAcceptUserOffset`; a vertical one is
  a primary scroll view, always scrollable).
- `div`: touch scrolls (`ScrollSource::Touch`) drag the axes with the recognizer's release velocity, and the
  recognizer's own momentum steps after the release are ignored (the container runs Flutter's simulation
  instead). Trackpad pans (pixel deltas with phases: Flutter's `PointerPanZoom`, which its drag recognizer takes)
  drag them too, with a velocity tracker per axis and `considerFling`; the platform's momentum after the release
  is ignored, as Flutter's macOS embedder skips it. Wheels stop a drag or fling and stay clamped (Flutter's
  `PointerScrollEvent`). A touch that lands on a container an axis of which is moving holds it
  (`HoldScrollActivity`): `Window::hold_scroll` (crate-private) marks its route, and the touch recognizer makes it a
  drag from its first pixel that never taps (`BallisticScrollActivity.shouldIgnorePointer`). Prepaint lays the
  axes out and ticks them, leaves an axis it is moving unclamped, requests frames while it animates, and moves each
  child as `StretchEffect`'s scale would move its centre (GPUI has no scale transform for every primitive, so
  sizes stay). Paint draws the glow per `GlowController::paint` (`_GlowingOverscrollIndicatorPainter`'s geometry,
  mirrored or transposed per edge) in Material 2's default secondary colour.
- gpui_macos: a trackpad scroll's `NSEventPhaseCancelled` (fingers rested, then lifted without scrolling) is
  `TouchPhase::Cancelled`, not `Moved`, so the pan it started ends.
- `TouchGestureRecognizer::fling_velocity` and `hold`, `GestureTuning::is_fling_gesture` (Flutter's
  `isFlingGesture`, shared by the recognizer and trackpad drags), `Window::gesture_tuning` /
  `touch_fling_velocity` (crate-private): the release velocity Flutter's `DragEndDetails` carries, read during
  the release's scroll step.
- Tests: `overscroll::scroll_position::tests::touch_overscroll_follows_flutter`, one row per behaviour, replaying
  touch, wheel and trackpad traces through the test platform with 0013's `play_trace`: bounce friction and spring
  back, a fling bouncing at the bottom, `None`, a per-container opt-out, stretch rising and receding, glow showing
  and fading, a wheel staying clamped, nested containers each overscrolling only on their own axis, an offset set
  during a fling, a touch holding a spring back (and releasing it), a held touch dragging from its first pixel
  without a tap, a trackpad pan bouncing, a trackpad fling with the platform's momentum ignored, rows removed
  during a fling, and a horizontal strip whose content fits.

Why: Parallax's platform behaviour table picks overscroll per target (bounce on iOS and macOS, stretch on
Android); GPUI's containers only clamp. The physics and indicators are already Flutter ports (0012, 0015), so this
patch is the glue Flutter's `ScrollPosition` is, ported the same way. It lives in `div.rs` because the scroll
offset, its clamp and the scroll listener are `Interactivity`'s private state; no extension trait can reach them.
It is over the 200-line guide: a Flutter port (exempt, AGENTS.md patch rule 4), and the glow painter, the stretch
and the position state are one concern (what a container does past its edge). Code ported from Flutter, Copyright
2014 The Flutter Authors, BSD-3-Clause; the new file names its sources at Flutter 3.50.1.

Upstream PR: not filed yet.

